The intersection of lines satisfying the two options is output.ocal motions
.BD \eu
and
.BD \ed .
To go back up the page half a point-size, insert a
.BD \eu
at the desired place;
to go down, insert a
.BD \ed .
.BD \eu \& (
and
.BD \ed
should always
be used in pairs, as explained below.)
Thus
.P1
Area = \e(*pr\eu2\ed
.P2
produces
.P1
Area = \(*pr\u2\d
.P2
To make the `2' smaller, bracket it with
.BD \es\-2...\es0 .
Since
.BD \eu
and
.BD \ed
refer to the current point size,
be sure to put them either both inside or both outside
the size changes,
or you will get an unbala\&\*.na
